			<div class="content" id="post-content-2187415">
									

<p>Andariel, I really like this! A couple of suggestions, out of love for the article:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Accessible <strong>entrances</strong> to SCP-2692 are monitored with video surveillance, while the <strong>entrances</strong> themselves are monitored by on-site personnel.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This sentence is kind of confusing. I assume that the second use of &quot;entrances&quot; refers to the &quot;locations&quot; or &quot;points&quot; themselves, rather than the entrances to the passageways that lead to those points? Maybe replace that with &quot;locations,&quot; &quot;points,&quot; or &quot;anomalies.&quot;</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Subject keeps scraps of paper in <strong>their</strong> pockets with information written in the form of pictograms.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I'd replace &quot;their&quot; with &quot;her.&quot; She's a non-anomalous human whose sex/gender we know, so no need for pronoun shenanigans. ;)</p>
<p>Otherwise, this is really good work. Happily upvoted! :)</p>

